 ## Overview
-GPU Raycasting is a direct volume rendering technique that generates 2D images from 3D data fields by casting rays through the volume, where the computation of resampling and image synthesis is processed by the GPU fragment shader for parallel calculation [S2][S3, unofficial]. It approximates the volume rendering integral by sampling at a finite number of positions along each ray [S1, unofficial]. It differs from its siblings [GPU-based Ray-casting for Unstructured Grids](1118-gpu-based-ray-casting-for-unstructured-grids.md) and [CUDA Volume Ray Casting](2048-cuda-volume-ray-casting.md) by typically relying on regular 3D textures and fixed-function pipeline optimizations (such as early ray termination) rather than complex unstructured grid traversal or explicit CUDA kernel management, though it shares the core principle of mapping rays to parallel execution units [G].
